    {
      "id": "ideas",
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Sorting ideas",
      "points": [
        "**Bubble sort**: repeated passes, swapping neighbours. Up to n − 1 passes, so **O(n²)**.",
        "**Selection sort** picks the minimum each pass; **insertion sort** slides each item into place. Both are O(n²).",
        "**Merge sort** splits the array in half, sorts each half and merges them: **O(n log n)**.",
        "In real code, `Arrays.sort(a)` or `Collections.sort(list)` do the job, but interviews ask you to know how."
      ],
      "pattern": "int t = a[i];\na[i] = a[j];\na[j] = t; // swap needs a temporary"
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "A classic trap. What does this print?",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int x = 3, y = 8;\n        x = y;\n        y = x;\n        System.out.println(x + \" \" + y);\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "8 8",
          "why": "Yes. The 3 was overwritten on the first line. Use int t = x; x = y; y = t;"
        },
        {
          "text": "3 8",
          "why": "The two assignments do change the values."
        },
        {
          "text": "3 3",
          "why": "x = y copies 8 into x first."
        },
        {
          "text": "8 3",
          "why": "After x = y, the old 3 is gone for good, so y = x copies 8 back. A swap needs a temporary variable."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 0
